$^2$\end{document} of 0.564, and a mean Pearson correlation coefficient of 0.749, outperforming existing approaches. Additional validate via case studies on supplementary Escherichia coli ABP sequences indicates that the MIC predictions of GGN-ABPMIC are closer to the true values, demonstrating strong predictive performance and generalization for ABP activity. The data and codes for GGN-ABPMIC are available at https://github.com/LiJinYu1231/GGN-ABPMIC/tree/master.

sec.p:
Research on predicting ABPs using artificial intelligence mainly covers two tasks: ABP classification and antibacterial activity prediction. Antibacterial activity is typically quantified by the minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC). A lower MIC indicates stronger inhibitory activity against pathogenic microorganisms. Accurate MIC prediction can accelerate the discovery and development of novel ABPs. With advances in computational biology and machine learning, researchers have increasingly applied these techniques to predict ABP activity [9]. Xiao et al. [10] proposed an MIC predictor based on five physicochemical properties of AMPs and pseudo-amino acid composition features, using a Gaussian kernel regression model to predict the inhibitory effect of AMPs on Escherichia coli. In addition, they observed a poor predictive correlation, which they attributed to the wide range of experimental MIC values. Li et al. [11] compared MIC predictions for Streptococcus pneumoniae isolates using traditional methods and machine learning, validating their results against broth microdilution data. They found that penicillin-binding protein sequences could effectively predict \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
