$\beta $\end{document}-lactam antibiotic MIC values and exhibit high consistency. Sharma et al. [12] developed ESKAPEE-MICpred, a dual-input model integrating transfer learning and ensemble learning to predict ABP activity against five pathogens. The model takes pathogen genomic data as one input and incorporates outputs from a pre-trained model, adjusting feature-extraction weights to improve predictions. Yan et al. [13] used deep learning, specifically multi-branch convolutional neural networks and attention mechanisms, to predict the MIC against E. coli, achieving better performance than traditional machine learning approaches. Yao et al. [14] proposed AMPActiPred, a three-stage ABP predictor: first, determine whether a peptide is an ABP; second, predict its specificity toward 10 bacterial species; third, estimate its activity level. Cai et al. [15] introduced a fine-tuned BERT-based Transformer model that extracts sequence embedding features and uses transfer learning to predict ABP activity on E. coli and Staphylococcus aureus datasets.

In this study, we compiled the ABP sequences targeting 10 bacterial species from AMPActiPred [14]. In addition to the sequences, we collected the corresponding MIC values and the 553-dimensional peptide feature descriptors. The data were obtained from the GRAMPA database [18], which contains ABP sequences of 10–30 amino acids and integrates several widely used ABP resources, including APD [8], DADP [19], DBaASP [20], DRAMP [21], and YADAMP [22]. All activity values were converted to pMIC (in \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
