$R^{2}$\end{document} of 0.547, and a PCC of 0.730, but it still underperformed compared with the full GGN-ABPMIC model. These results indicate that MDH-Loss is more suitable for ABP MIC prediction than the conventional alternatives. In particular, although Huber loss shows competitive optimization behavior, the final predictive performance obtained with MDH-Loss remains superior, supporting its effectiveness in the GGN-ABPMIC framework.

